import{_ as t,o as i,c as a,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.BM5YPQmL.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"3.0.0-WP-tui-input-encoding-hardening — TUI input and encoding hardening","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"release/3.0.0-WP-tui-input-encoding-hardening.md","filePath":"release/3.0.0-WP-tui-input-encoding-hardening.md"}'),r={name:"release/3.0.0-WP-tui-input-encoding-hardening.md"};function o(d,e,s,c,l,h){return i(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="_3-0-0-wp-tui-input-encoding-hardening-—-tui-input-and-encoding-hardening" tabindex="-1">3.0.0-WP-tui-input-encoding-hardening — TUI input and encoding hardening <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-0-0-wp-tui-input-encoding-hardening-—-tui-input-and-encoding-hardening" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.0.0-WP-tui-input-encoding-hardening — TUI input and encoding hardening&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The input/encoding/display hardening batch: five user-reported defects driven to root cause with reproduction evidence on every one, plus the full three-chain sweep behind them. Every fix follows the &quot;standard discipline, no per-platform special-casing&quot; rule.</p><h2 id="changes" tabindex="-1">Changes <a class="header-anchor" href="#changes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Changes&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="_1-tui-entry-hiding-registered-unlisted" tabindex="-1">1. TUI entry hiding (registered, unlisted) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-tui-entry-hiding-registered-unlisted" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. TUI entry hiding (registered, unlisted)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The eight legacy TUI sub-launchers (<code>tui-test</code>, <code>tui-hw</code>, <code>tui-net</code>, <code>tui-sysinfo</code>, <code>tui-time</code>, <code>tui-drv</code>, <code>tui-sys</code>, <code>tui-ext</code>) stay registered and directly executable - only their LISTING is gone: <code>help</code> and <code>help -a</code> no longer show them, the desktop tree lost the Extensions lab mirror node, and the reconciled calibers are 311 registered / 292 listed / 8 hidden (the presentation gates assert both numbers). No code path was removed; the single fact source is the <code>k_hidden[]</code> table in <code>l1_tui_entry_filter.c</code>.</p><h3 id="_2-the-ocire-space-capture-root-cause-a-scan-code-bounds-overrun" tabindex="-1">2. The OCIRE space capture (root cause: a scan-code bounds overrun) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-the-ocire-space-capture-root-cause-a-scan-code-bounds-overrun" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. The OCIRE space capture (root cause: a scan-code bounds overrun)&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>lang zh</code> typed as <code>LANGZH</code>, every spaced command broken: the PS/2 scan-code decoder indexed a table one entry past its end - the space bar&#39;s make code (0x39) fell off and read adjacent memory as the ASCII mapping. Fixed at the table; the six-path matrix (OCIRE and OCOS x serial, PS/2, UEFI) is all green, <code>LANG ZH</code>, <code>LANG EN</code> and every spaced command included.</p><h3 id="_3-the-cross-write-utf-8-tty" tabindex="-1">3. The cross-write UTF-8 TTY <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-the-cross-write-utf-8-tty" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. The cross-write UTF-8 TTY&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The ush line editor echoed each key event as a one-byte write; a pasted or IME-committed CJK glyph arrives as 3-4 such writes and the console decoded each independently - a lone lead byte painted as a raw Latin-1 cell, every hanzi echoed as solid blocks until a full-line redraw happened to fix it. The TTY now reassembles multi-byte sequences across writes (<code>core_tty_utf8.c</code>): receive and echo are byte-exact, <code>cat</code> of a UTF-8 file returns the exact sequence, backspace deletes a WHOLE character (the 2-cell wipe on the wire), the 80-cell wrap never splits a glyph, and advance is per-CHARACTER. VLM-read VGA screendumps + raw-byte hexdumps in the evidence. Not in scope (unchanged by design): input methods, a locale system, filename normalization.</p><h3 id="_4-serial-encoding-direction-verdict-no-code-change" tabindex="-1">4. Serial encoding direction (verdict, no code change) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-serial-encoding-direction-verdict-no-code-change" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. Serial encoding direction (verdict, no code change)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Raw-byte captures of both serial paths: the kernel output is uniformly valid UTF-8, OCBL emits pure ASCII + standard ANSI CSI (the same sequences GRUB&#39;s serial menu emits), and the built-in <code>serial_encoding=gbk</code> tee converts correctly in both directions. The reported &quot;COM1 garbled output&quot; is the host terminal decoding - the reporter&#39;s Windows host runs codepage windows-936 (GBK) reading a UTF-8 stream. Registered, not a code defect.</p><h3 id="_5-the-vmware-triple-fault-fix-five-findings-four-code-fixes" tabindex="-1">5. The VMware triple-fault fix (five findings, four code fixes) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-the-vmware-triple-fault-fix-five-findings-four-code-fixes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. The VMware triple-fault fix (five findings, four code fixes)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The delivered <code>vmware.log</code> (15 <code>Triple fault.</code> events, ~1.24 s per cycle) and boot serial were driven to a full reasoning chain (the verbatim quotes, the crash-phase mapping and the hypothesis matrix are in <code>evidence/.../vmware/vmware-reasoning.md</code>):</p><ul><li><strong>H1 - the OCBL core never loaded an IDT.</strong> The 64-bit core inherited the firmware&#39;s real-mode IVT state (<code>{0, 0x3FF}</code>), which misdecodes any vectoring in long mode - an asynchronous event escalated #GP -&gt; #DF -&gt; triple fault. OCBL now builds its own 256-gate table (<code>ocbl_idt.c/.S/.h</code>): exceptions report on serial and halt, IRQs/NMIs iretq; the real-mode trampoline swaps the vectoring discipline at every mode boundary (the firmware IVT going down, our table coming back up). Proven deterministically without VMware: an NMI latched at a breakpoint in pure 64-bit OCBL code kills the pre-fix build (the reset path) and is absorbed by the fixed one.</li><li><strong>H2 - the El Torito boot drive number is firmware-chosen</strong> (the reporter&#39;s VMware passes 0x9F, not 0xE0+): the scan never saw the boot CD, the menu fell back to the Shutdown countdown. Gated on the El Torito flavor now.</li><li><strong>H3 - the state monitor&#39;s EBDA guard read I/O ports instead of the BDA memory word</strong>, so it never fired and the state pages could land in BIOS-private low memory (the &quot;next INT call triple-faults&quot; class). Forced-build proof: the guard fires, the kernel honors <code>oc.stmon=off</code>, the boot completes.</li><li><strong>H4 - the page-table relocation for low-EBDA machines targeted the trampoline&#39;s own address</strong> and the return path hardcoded CR3. Relocation target moved with a single-source-of-truth publish; forced-build matrix: the new target boots, the old one dies on both pre-fix and post-fix builds.</li><li>Plus: CMOS register selects keep the NMI mask set, and the UEFI getkey accepts the CSI-arrow tails OVMF&#39;s ConIn can steal.</li></ul><h3 id="_6-the-extended-sweep" tabindex="-1">6. The extended sweep <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-the-extended-sweep" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6. The extended sweep&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Re-audit of the input/encoding/display chains on the final tree. The BIOS menu&#39;s serial keying was dead (the int 14h route never delivers under SeaBIOS) - now reading the 16550 directly, verified live. A regression of this very batch (installing our IDT under UEFI kills OVMF&#39;s interrupt-driven drivers) was caught and fixed before shipping - the IDT install is BIOS-flavor only. The OVMF menu input starvation is registered as K-06 with workarounds.</p><h2 id="verification-all-on-the-release-build" tabindex="-1">Verification (all on the release build) <a class="header-anchor" href="#verification-all-on-the-release-build" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Verification (all on the release build)&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>make all</code>: 0 errors, 0 compile warnings (1 documented ld RWX note); reproducible-check PASS (byte-identical across two clean builds)</li><li>31/31 runtime regression (command-count floor 292 - the listed caliber after the hiding)</li><li>WP-08 driver-framework gate: 43/43</li><li>dhtest: 5/5 correctness + determinism IDENTICAL</li><li>SSH E2E: ALL PASS; HTTPS E2E: 8/8</li><li>verify_presentation: ALL CHECKS PASS (dictionary 311/292 calibers, fact-source, privacy, exemptions)</li><li>BIOS + UEFI boots: banner <code>3.0.0-WP-tui-input-encoding-hardening</code> build 478 + ring-3 ush&gt; + boot complete</li><li>The space matrix (six paths) and the UTF-8 chain (7/7) re-run green on the final tree</li></ul><h2 id="known-issues" tabindex="-1">Known issues <a class="header-anchor" href="#known-issues" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Known issues&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>K-06 - the OCBL menu cannot be driven from the keyboard under OVMF while boot services are live.</strong> No input source delivers (serial bytes are drained by OVMF&#39;s terminal driver and never resurface through ConIn; PS/2 equally starved) - the menu auto-boots the default entry after the 10 s countdown. The BIOS flavor&#39;s menu is fully keyable (serial + PS/2). Candidate future fix: a WaitForEvent-based menu loop.</li><li>K-01/K-02/K-03/K-05 carry over unchanged (see KNOWN_ISSUES).</li></ul><p>Evidence: <code>evidence/wp-tui-input-encoding-hardening/</code> (the serial raw-byte captures and hexdump analysis, the VMware reasoning chain with all test transcripts, the sweep findings, the regression gate captures).</p>',21)])])}const g=t(r,[["render",o]]);export{p as __pageData,g as default};
